<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Albert III, Duke of Bavaria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1401">1401</a>–<a href="/years/1460">1460</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Albert the Pious · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">House of Wittelsbach</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Albert III was duke of Bavaria-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> from 1438 to 1460. He is remembered for his secret marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agnes-bernauer">Agnes Bernauer</a>, a commoner whom his father Duke Ernest had drowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/straubing">Straubing</a> in 1435. He later ruled peacefully, inheriting part of Bavaria-Straubing, and his son Albert IV reunited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albert-iii-duke-of-bavaria.jpg" alt="Albert III, Duke of Bavaria" width="320" height="470" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rijksmuseum · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portret_van_Albrecht_III,_hertog_van_Beieren,_RP-P-2016-740.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Albert was born on 27 March 1401, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-duke-of-bavaria">Ernest, Duke of Bavaria</a>, and Elisabetta Visconti, a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernabo-visconti">Bernabò Visconti</a>. In 1429 he was engaged to Elisabeth, the daughter of Eberhard III, Count of Württemberg, but she eloped with Count John IV of Werdenberg, who had been a page at her father's court.</p><p>In 1432, while Albert was administrator for his father in the former duchy of Bavaria-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/straubing">Straubing</a>, he secretly married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agnes-bernauer">Agnes Bernauer</a>, a maid from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a>. His father opposed the marriage. In 1435 Duke Ernest ordered her to be murdered: she was accused of witchcraft and thrown into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> while Albert was away hunting. Albert then stayed with Louis VII, Duke of Bavaria-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingolstadt">Ingolstadt</a>, at Ingolstadt, but he was reconciled with his father in November of that year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After the reconciliation Albert married Anna of Brunswick-Grubenhagen-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/einbeck">Einbeck</a>, daughter of Duke Eric I of Brunswick-Grubenhagen, on 22 January 1437. They had ten children. In 1438 he succeeded his father as duke of Bavaria-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, and around 1438 to 1439 he turned Blutenburg Castle, set between two arms of the River Würm, into a hunting lodge. His third son Sigismund later extended it.</p><p>In 1440 Albert refused the offer of the Bohemian crown. In 1442 he expelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> from all the Upper Bavarian territories, and Jewish settlement was not permitted there again for about 250 years. In 1444 and 1445 he led two campaigns against the robber barons. When the line of the dukes of Bavaria-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingolstadt">Ingolstadt</a> died out, he released that duchy in 1447 to his father's cousin Henry XVI of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria-landshut">Bavaria-Landshut</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1455 Albert founded the Benedictine monastery at Andechs. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> on 29 February 1460 and was buried at Andechs. Of his children, John IV, Ernest and Albert died young or in early adulthood, while Albert IV, born in 1447, later became duke. Sigismund lived at Blutenburg, Margaret married Federico I Gonzaga in Mantua in 1463, and Elisabeth married Elector Ernst of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> in 1460. Albert also had at least three illegitimate children.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Albert III of Bavaria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Duke Ernest had Agnes Bernauer, the commoner wife of his son <b>Albert III</b>, drowned at Straubing in 1435.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-bavaria-munich">Duchy of Bavaria-Munich</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.14,11.58&amp;year=1439" aria-label="Munich on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Albert III, Duke of Bavaria"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5656 5950h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5656 5950h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.14,11.58&amp;year=1439">What was here in 1439? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
